{
  "id": "r06",
  "text": "The notice said distribution would end on April twenty ninth. Keep that date separate from the cancellation, and from what happened next. Removing a store listing and preventing an existing customer from downloading again are different changes. At first, previous downloaders could still reach P.T. through their history. Reporting on May sixth described that route failing in North America too. This is why the actual error screen matters. The issue wasn't simply that somebody had searched for a game too late. The expected recovery path had failed. A copy already installed on a PlayStation Four could still work; an empty drive could no longer be treated as a temporary inconvenience. Konami did not remotely erase every surviving installation. The software continued to exist, but the ordinary way of obtaining it had become unreliable or unavailable. A game distributed as a free digital download suddenly depended on a particular physical machine keeping particular files. That's a very different relationship with a console from assuming everything in your library can always be replaced.",
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
